WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.658
Task Analysis.

2
00:00:01.658 --> 00:00:06.751
ID 553 - Module 3: Examining the Situation.

3
00:00:06.751 --> 00:00:13.857
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:14.457 --> 00:00:23.273
Task analysis answers one question: what, exactly, must the
learner be able to do, and what does that performance
consist of?

5
00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:33.994
It turns a goal like 'nurses will administer medications
safely in the new system' into a map of the knowledge,
skills, and decisions the goal requires.

6
00:00:33.994 --> 00:00:40.341
That map determines your objectives, your sequence, your
strategies, and your assessments.

7
00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:47.676
Without it, designers teach what they happen to know rather
than what the performance actually requires.

8
00:00:48.276 --> 00:00:59.752
Start by classifying the goal using Gagne's outcome types,
because the type determines the analysis method and, later,
the instructional conditions.

9
00:00:59.752 --> 00:01:05.489
Verbal information is stating and recalling; you analyze it
by clustering.

10
00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:15.026
Intellectual skills are discriminating, classifying,
applying rules, and solving problems; you analyze them
hierarchically.

11
00:01:15.026 --> 00:01:19.989
Cognitive strategies are how learners manage their own
thinking.

12
00:01:19.989 --> 00:01:25.261
Motor skills are physical procedures; you analyze them
procedurally.

13
00:01:25.261 --> 00:01:26.967
Attitudes are choices.

14
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:35.883
Most real goals combine types: a procedure with decisions at
branch points and information needed at several steps.

15
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:39.485
Let's do a procedural analysis live.

16
00:01:39.485 --> 00:01:44.822
The goal: administer a medication with bedside barcode
scanning.

17
00:01:44.822 --> 00:01:48.074
Step one, scan the patient's wristband.

18
00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:50.825
If it reads and matches, proceed.

19
00:01:50.825 --> 00:01:56.829
If it doesn't read, rescan; after two failures, use the
manual override.

20
00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:06.668
The override is its own sub-procedure: enter the record
number, confirm two identifiers verbally, document the
reason.

21
00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:09.836
Step two, scan the medication barcode.

22
00:02:09.836 --> 00:02:16.507
If a mismatch alert appears, stop, do not administer, follow
the alert workflow.

23
00:02:16.507 --> 00:02:20.843
Notice what the analysis exposed: the branch points.

24
00:02:20.843 --> 00:02:32.350
That's where errors happen, where practice should
concentrate, and where the expert performs automatically and
would never think to teach.

25
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.067
Now a hierarchical analysis for an intellectual skill.

26
00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:41.488
Take the decision: does a mismatch alert require stopping?

27
00:02:41.488 --> 00:02:46.977
Ask: what must the learner already be able to do in order to
learn this?

28
00:02:46.977 --> 00:02:49.645
They must interpret the alert type.

29
00:02:49.645 --> 00:02:57.650
Ask again: to interpret the type, they must distinguish
wrong-patient, wrong-drug, and wrong-time alerts.

30
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:04.511
Ask again: to distinguish them, they must read the alert
display, which is an entry skill.

31
00:03:04.511 --> 00:03:12.135
Each answer becomes a subordinate skill below the one above,
and lower skills must be learned first.

32
00:03:12.135 --> 00:03:16.023
You stop when you reach what learners already have.

33
00:03:16.623 --> 00:03:17.897
Two more methods.

34
00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:28.242
For verbal information, cluster the items into meaningful
sets by function, location, or time; clusters often turn
directly into job aids.

35
00:03:28.242 --> 00:03:31.766
And for complex tasks, cognitive task analysis.

36
00:03:31.766 --> 00:03:35.664
Experts make decisions they can't easily articulate.

37
00:03:35.664 --> 00:03:49.383
Structured interviews, think-aloud protocols, and critical-
incident recall surface the cues experts attend to, the
decisions they make, and the errors novices make at the same
points.

38
00:03:49.383 --> 00:04:02.352
Full cognitive task analysis is expensive, but even a light
version, asking an expert to narrate a recent difficult case
and probing at each decision, improves any analysis.

39
00:04:02.352 --> 00:04:10.073
The research is clear that instruction built on it
outperforms instruction built on expert self-report.

40
00:04:10.673 --> 00:04:14.190
Draw a line across the bottom of your analysis.

41
00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:18.829
Everything below it is assumed; everything above it is
taught.

42
00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:28.033
Where you draw the line comes from the learner analysis, and
if learners vary widely, you may need two lines and two
paths.

43
00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.829
Then check the analysis.

44
00:04:29.829 --> 00:04:34.992
Completeness: could a novice perform the goal using only
what's here?

45
00:04:34.992 --> 00:04:37.836
Have an expert and a novice review it.

46
00:04:37.836 --> 00:04:46.291
Accuracy: observe the task performed, because analyses built
from documentation miss workarounds and tacit steps.

47
00:04:46.291 --> 00:04:55.495
And make every branch point explicit with its common errors;
those errors become practice items and assessment
distractors.

48
00:04:56.095 --> 00:05:13.014
The common mistakes: listing topics instead of performances;
stopping too early so the first objective is unteachable in
one lesson; teaching the happy path with no decisions or
error handling; and analyzing from documentation without
observing anyone.

49
00:05:13.014 --> 00:05:30.939
For assignment six, choose one goal from your project,
classify it, decompose it with the right method to at least
three levels, number every element, mark the entry-skill
line using your learner analysis, and have at least one
person who performs the task review it.

50
00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:38.325
Then write one paragraph on what the analysis revealed that
you would not have taught from your own knowledge.

51
00:05:38.325 --> 00:05:43.091
That paragraph is usually the most interesting thing in the
assignment.
